Architecture plan for issues #65-#67

Project shape
- Static browser-only camera capture app using plain HTML, CSS, and ES modules.
- Runtime code lives under `js/`; tests live under `tests/` and use Vitest with
  `happy-dom` plus mocked browser APIs.
- No backend database is needed for this batch. Captured media continues to be stored
  locally through the existing `js/storage.js` path.

Relevant modules
- `js/capture.js`
  - Owns `capturePicture(videoEl)` and `recordVideo(stream, options)`.
  - `recordVideo` currently starts a `MediaRecorder`, auto-stops at `maxSeconds`, collects
    chunks, converts the final `Blob` to a data URL, and resolves with
    `{ dataUrl, duration, mimeType }`.
  - Issue #65 should extend this function with `options.onStart`, called once the
    recorder is recording, with a stable `{ stop }` handle.
- `js/app.js`
  - Owns app event wiring, recording state, countdown ticks, control enable/disable
    state, and saving video items through `addItem`.
  - Issue #66 should keep the record button enabled while recording, switch its
    visible/ARIA label to Stop, wire it to the stop handle from `recordVideo`, and reset
    UI state in the existing recording cleanup path.
- `styles.css`
  - Owns button and recording indicator styling.
  - Issue #66 may need one focused active-recording button style if no existing class
    can express the Stop state clearly.
- `tests/video-recorder.test.js`
  - Unit coverage for `recordVideo` behavior and MediaRecorder stubs.
  - Issue #67 should add early-stop coverage here.
- `tests/app-record-button.test.js`
  - UI wiring coverage for record button state and saved video items.
  - Issue #67 should extend this for Record-to-Stop label/state behavior.
- `tests/app-recording.test.js`
  - Recording guard/countdown tests for `runRecordingTask`.
  - Issue #67 should cover the end-to-end app flow around early stop and duration.

Issue dependencies
- #65 is the foundation. The app cannot stop recording early until `recordVideo` exposes
  a safe handle.
- #66 depends on #65. The UI should store the current stop handle while a recording is
  active and call it when the same button is clicked in Stop mode.
- #67 depends on #65 and #66. Add focused tests after the implementation shape is known,
  using the existing MediaRecorder/app mocks rather than adding new libraries.

Key design decisions
- Keep the public `recordVideo` contract backward compatible:
  `recordVideo(stream, { maxSeconds })` must keep working.
- Add only one new option, `onStart`, and call it after `recorder.start()` has put the
  recorder into the recording state.
- The stop handle should be idempotent. Calling `stop()` more than once, or after the
  recorder is already inactive, should not throw or finalize chunks twice.
- Duration should use actual elapsed wall-clock time for manual stops, while auto-stop
  remains capped by `maxSeconds`.
- Preserve the existing single-recording guard in `app.js`; do not allow a second
  recording to start while one is active.
- Keep UI state cleanup centralized in the existing `finally` path so manual stop,
  auto-stop, and recorder errors all reset controls consistently.
